WEBVTT

00:03.880 --> 00:06.360
Okay, so now what is exactly a full join?

00:06.360 --> 00:11.000
If you use it, SQL can returns everything all the rows from both tables.

00:11.000 --> 00:15.960
So now if you check again our circles from the left table, we want to get everything all the rows.

00:15.960 --> 00:17.600
So you will get the whole circle.

00:17.600 --> 00:22.720
And as well from the right table you want to get everything all the rows, the whole circle, so that

00:22.720 --> 00:27.720
you want to get everything the matching, the unmatching, all the data from left and right.

00:27.760 --> 00:28.960
Now let's check the syntax.

00:28.960 --> 00:30.240
It's going to be very simple.

00:30.240 --> 00:33.240
The join type here is going to be a full join and the full join.

00:33.240 --> 00:35.200
It is very similar to the inner join.

00:35.200 --> 00:39.040
You remember the order of the tables is not important at all.

00:39.040 --> 00:41.840
So there is here no main table and secondary table.

00:41.880 --> 00:46.160
Both of the tables are important and it doesn't matter in your query where you start.

00:46.160 --> 00:50.840
You can start from a full join B or you can start from B, then full join A.

00:50.880 --> 00:52.640
You will get the exact same results.

00:52.640 --> 00:53.600
It sounds simple.

00:53.640 --> 00:56.200
Let's go to SQL and practice the full join.

00:56.240 --> 00:56.560
All right.

00:56.560 --> 00:57.800
So now we have the following task.

00:57.800 --> 01:02.480
And it says get all customers and all orders even if there is no match.

01:02.480 --> 01:05.400
So now again we need the data from customers and orders.

01:05.400 --> 01:07.560
But now of course which type we're going to use.

01:07.600 --> 01:09.480
It says even if there is no match.

01:09.480 --> 01:13.020
But it didn't say no much from orders or customers.

01:13.020 --> 01:17.260
So you can understand from this task we are not focusing only on the orders or the customers.

01:17.260 --> 01:20.780
Both of them are equally important and we need all the data.

01:20.780 --> 01:25.900
So that means we need all the data from left, all the data from right, and we can go and use the full

01:25.900 --> 01:26.220
join.

01:26.220 --> 01:27.980
So now we have this query over here.

01:27.980 --> 01:31.180
We are starting from customers and then joining to orders.

01:31.180 --> 01:34.620
But now instead of having left we're going to say full join.

01:34.620 --> 01:36.820
So now let's go and just execute it.

01:36.860 --> 01:41.020
Now if you are looking to the left side you can see we are getting all the customers right.

01:41.020 --> 01:42.660
So we have our five customers.

01:42.660 --> 01:46.020
And if you are looking to the right you can see all our orders.

01:46.020 --> 01:49.420
So with that we have everything from left and everything from right.

01:49.420 --> 01:52.980
And the matching data is just side by side in the results.

01:52.980 --> 01:55.140
And if there is no match, we are getting nulls.

01:55.140 --> 01:56.980
So actually with that, we have solved the task.

01:56.980 --> 02:03.020
And again it doesn't matter how you start, you can start from the orders and then join it to the customers

02:03.020 --> 02:05.340
and you will get the exact same results.

02:05.340 --> 02:07.180
So you are getting exactly the same data.

02:07.220 --> 02:10.860
Now let's go and understand exactly how SQL executed the full join.

02:10.900 --> 02:14.500
Okay, again we have the data of the customers and the orders and our full join.

02:14.500 --> 02:18.420
So now SQL can identify those columns that we want to see in the results.

02:18.420 --> 02:23.600
So the ID and the first name, the order ID and the sales and formations to the output.

02:23.640 --> 02:27.240
Now it's going to start from the left table since it is started with the customers.

02:27.280 --> 02:31.640
It's going to take simply everything from the left table and present it in the output.

02:31.680 --> 02:35.960
Since it is fully joined, we want to see all the data from the left side and now start searching for

02:36.000 --> 02:37.640
matches from the right table.

02:37.640 --> 02:43.480
So let's start with the first customer and as usual we will get the order from the customer.

02:43.480 --> 02:44.320
Number one.

02:44.320 --> 02:48.000
And the same thing for the second customer we have as well here match.

02:48.000 --> 02:49.240
So we will get as well.

02:49.240 --> 02:50.600
It's like the left join.

02:50.600 --> 02:54.720
And for the third one we have as well a match and we're going to have it like this.

02:54.760 --> 03:00.440
And since we don't have orders for those two customers, we will get as well nulls in the outputs.

03:00.440 --> 03:02.120
So SQL going to mark it with null.

03:02.160 --> 03:03.480
The same thing over here.

03:04.120 --> 03:06.120
And as well for the last customer.

03:06.120 --> 03:08.440
So we will get nulls for those two customers.

03:08.480 --> 03:10.800
And now of course with SQL will not stop here.

03:10.800 --> 03:12.880
Otherwise we will get a left join effect.

03:12.920 --> 03:18.440
Now SQL can start looking at the right side to find any order that is not in the output.

03:18.440 --> 03:23.680
So SQL can see okay, the first order is in the output, the second one is as well in the output the

03:23.680 --> 03:26.440
third, but the fourth one is not in the results.

03:26.440 --> 03:32.740
So SQL is going to take this result and put it in the outputs, so this order has no match at all from

03:32.740 --> 03:33.420
the left side.

03:33.460 --> 03:37.540
And with that, if you are looking to the right side, you can see this girl gonna be happy because

03:37.540 --> 03:40.340
we have all the orders from the right table.

03:40.380 --> 03:42.380
And of course SQL will not leave it like this.

03:42.420 --> 03:48.220
Instead of that, SQL gonna show nulls on the left side so there is no ID and there is no first name.

03:48.220 --> 03:50.540
So this is exactly why we got this results.

03:50.540 --> 03:53.980
And this is how SQL executed the full join.

03:54.020 --> 03:54.220
Okay.

03:54.260 --> 03:59.620
So now if you are looking to the use cases I can say you can use the full join in order as well to recombine

03:59.620 --> 04:01.540
the data from multiple tables.

04:01.540 --> 04:07.060
If you don't want to miss anything from all four tables, all data, the matching and unmatching data,

04:07.060 --> 04:09.580
but I don't use it usually for data enrichment.

04:09.580 --> 04:14.980
For the second use case, and where we can use the full join is in the last use case as well, but with

04:14.980 --> 04:16.820
a little twist that we're going to learn later.

04:16.820 --> 04:19.540
So this is mainly where we can use the full join.

04:19.540 --> 04:19.900
All right.

04:19.900 --> 04:25.140
So with that we have covered the basic types of joins inner left right and full join.

04:25.180 --> 04:28.660
Those are the classical joins on how to combine two tables.

04:31.860 --> 04:34.980
Now we're going to start talking about the advanced SQL joins.

